    transliteration: "arjuna uvācha |\nprakṛtiṁ puruṣaṁ caiva\nkṣetraṁ kṣetra-jñam eva ca |\netad veditum icchāmi\njñānaṁ jñeyaṁ ca keśava ||1||",
    wordMeanings: "अर्जुनः उवाच (arjunaḥ uvācha) = अर्जुन ने कहा (Arjuna said); प्रकृतिम् (prakṛtim) = प्रकृति / जड़ प्रकृति को (Nature / material energy); पुरुषम् (puruṣam) = पुरुष / चेतन आत्मा को (the enjoyer / conscious Spirit); च एव (caiva) = और भी (and indeed); क्षेत्रम् (kṣetram) = क्षेत्र / शरीर रूपी कर्म-भूमि को (the Field / the body); क्षेत्र-ज्ञम् (kṣetra-jñam) = क्षेत्रज्ञ / शरीर को जानने वाले ज्ञाता को (the Knower of the Field / the soul); एव च (eva ca) = और (and also); एतत् (etat) = इन सबको (all this); वेदितुम् (veditum) = तत्व से जानने के लिए (to know / comprehend); इच्छामि (icchāmi) = मैं इच्छा करता हूँ (I desire); ज्ञानम् (jñānam) = ज्ञान क्या है (what is knowledge); ज्ञेयम् (jñeyam) = और जानने योग्य परम सत्य क्या है (and what is the object of knowledge); च (ca) = तथा (and); केशव (keśava) = हे केशव! (O Keshav!).",
    hindiMeaning: "अर्जुन ने कहा — हे केशव! मैं प्रकृति और पुरुष को, क्षेत्र (शरीर) और क्षेत्रज्ञ (आत्मा/ज्ञाता) को, तथा ज्ञान और ज्ञेय (जानने योग्य तत्व) को तत्वतः जानना चाहता हूँ!\n\nविस्तृत व्याख्या: **गीता के ज्ञान-षटक (अध्याय 13 से 18) का भव्य शुभारंभ**:\nगीता के 18 अध्यायों को तीन षटकों में विभाजित माना गया है — कर्म-षटक (अध्याय 1-6), भक्ति-षटक (अध्याय 7-12), और अब ज्ञान-षटक (अध्याय 13-18)। अर्जुन यहाँ संपूर्ण ब्रह्मांडीय तत्व-मीमांसा के 6 मूल प्रश्नों को एक साथ प्रस्तुत करता है:\n1. प्रकृति (Material Energy) क्या है?\n2. पुरुष (Conscious Soul) क्या है?\n3. क्षेत्र (The Field / शरीर) क्या है?\n4. क्षेत्रज्ञ (The Knower / आत्मा) कौन है?\n5. सच्चा ज्ञान (Real Knowledge) क्या है?\n6. ज्ञेय (The Ultimate Object of Knowledge) क्या है?\nयह श्लोक आत्म-साक्षात्कार की सबसे गहरी दार्शनिक जिज्ञासा का प्रतिनिधित्व करता है।",
    englishMeaning: "Arjuna said: O Keshava, I wish to know in truth about Prakriti (nature) and Purusha (the conscious Spirit), the Kshetra (the Field) and the Kshetragna (the Knower of the Field), as well as Knowledge (Jnana) and that which is to be known (Jneya)!\n\nPhilosophical Commentary: Chapter 13 inaugurates the third and final sextet of the Bhagavad Gita—the Jnana-Shatka (Chapters 13–18), focusing on metaphysical discernment and ultimate liberation:\n1. The Six Metaphysical Inquiries: Arjuna presents the six cardinal categories of classical Samkhya-Vedanta metaphysics:\n   - Prakṛti: The mutable, material cosmos and its energetic matrix.\n   - Puruṣa: The immutable conscious principle and transcendent witness.\n   - Kṣetra: The somatic and psychological field (the body-mind instrument).\n   - Kṣetrajña: The knowing subject that observes and illumines the field.\n   - Jñāna: The transformative process of spiritual perception and discernment.\n   - Jñeya: The ultimate Reality that is to be realized (Parabrahman).\n2. Transition from Devotion to Wisdom: Having established loving devotion in Chapter 12, Sri Krishna now equips Arjuna with the sharp sword of metaphysical discrimination (Viveka) to cut through the knot of material entanglement.",
    lifeLesson: "True intellectual and spiritual growth begins with clarifying fundamental questions: 'Who am I? What is this body-mind instrument? What is the world? And what is the highest purpose of existence?' Cultivate a keen thirst for self-inquiry rather than settling for superficial worldly assumptions."
